## Why This Is Asked

Tests for optimistic UI and offline resilience are common in messaging apps. This question probes ability to design deterministic E2E tests that cover front-end and back-end coordination, error paths, and data isolation.

## Key Concepts

- Optimistic UI updates
- Network fault simulation and retry handling
- Test data isolation and deterministic seeding
- Front-end/back-end state reconciliation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Playwright test snippet (simplified)
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('delete with optimistic update and Undo', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('https://chat.app');
  await page.fill('#newMessage', 'hello world');
  await page.click('#send');
  const msg = page.locator('.message').last();
  await expect(msg).toHaveText('hello world');

  await msg.click('.delete');
  await expect(page.locator('.toast-undo')).toBeVisible();
  await expect(page.locator('.message')).not.toContainText('hello world');

  await page.click('.toast-undo');
  await expect(page.locator('.message')).toContainText('hello world');
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to handle multiple concurrent deletions in a single channel?
- How would you verify backend state during intermittent connectivity without flaky tests?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests for multi-tenant data isolation are essential even at beginner level. The scenario exercises end-to-end thinking: setup, onboarding flow, and strict cross-tenant boundaries. It also introduces practical trade-offs like environment seeding and mocking external services.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant data isolation
- Test data seeding via API or fixtures
- DNS/subdomain routing mock
- External service mocking (email, auth)
- UI and API assertions for isolation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright skeleton
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('tenant isolation signup', async ({ page, request }) => {
  await request.post('/api/test/seed-tenant', { data: { name: 'TenantA', subdomain: 'tenant-a' }});
  await page.goto('https://tenant-a.example.com/signup');
  await page.fill('#org-name', 'Tenant A');
  await page.click('#next');
  //... complete 3 onboarding steps
  await expect(page).toHaveURL(/tenant-a/);
  // verify isolation
  const res = await request.get('/api/tenant/data', { headers: { 'X-TI-tenant': 'tenant-a' } });
  expect(res.ok()).toBe(true);
  const cross = await request.get('/api/tenant/data', { headers: { 'X-TI-tenant': 'tenant-b' } });
  expect(cross.status()).toBe(403);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you run these tests in parallel across multiple tenants while maintaining isolation?
- How would you validate data isolation after role-based access controls are updated?

